{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drugs is set to come to an end as a new law takes effect, making it a crime once again to possess small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, gives individuals caught with illicit substances the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines. Those found with personal-use possession will face a misdemeanor charge, punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\nThe law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain locations, such as parks. While the law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, it does not make it mandatory, leaving some to worry that it may create a patchwork of policies that vary from county to county.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day noted that the criteria for deflection are narrow, requiring individuals to meet specific requirements, including having no other charges or warrants, and being medically stable. If eligible, officers will connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes. If the team is unable to make contact within the time frame, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\nThe new law has sparked debate, with some arguing that it will create a more equitable system, while others worry that it will lead to confusion and unequal treatment.\n\n***", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.02678580768406391}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.11135436594486237}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.46469035744667053}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -1.775070309638977}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -